.TH DKIM_FUNCTIONS 3 "" "indimail-mta" "Library Functions"
.SH NAME
dkim_sign_new, dkim_sign_process, dkim_sign_domain, dkim_sign_get, dkim_sign_free,
dkim_verify_new, dkim_verify_process, dkim_verify_finish, dkim_verify_author_domain,
dkim_verify_status, dkim_verify_count, dkim_verify_free, dkim_status_text, dkim_code_name, dkim_free,
exit_if_old \- sign and verify mail with DKIM (RFC 6376, RFC 8463)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include \(dqdkim_functions.h\(dq
.sp
.B DKIMSign   *dkim_sign_new(const DKIMSignOptions *\fIopts\fB);
.B int         dkim_sign_process(DKIMSign *\fIs\fB, const char *\fIbuf\fB, size_t \fIlen\fB);
.B const char *dkim_sign_domain(DKIMSign *\fIs\fB);
.B int         dkim_sign_get(DKIMSign *\fIs\fB, const DKIMSignOptions *\fIopts\fB,
.B                           const char *\fIprivkey_pem\fB, char **\fIsig\fB);
.B void        dkim_sign_free(DKIMSign *\fIs\fB);
.sp
.B DKIMVerify *dkim_verify_new(const DKIMVerifyOptions *\fIopts\fB);
.B int         dkim_verify_process(DKIMVerify *\fIv\fB, const char *\fIbuf\fB, size_t \fIlen\fB);
.B int         dkim_verify_finish(DKIMVerify *\fIv\fB, int *\fInsigs\fB,
.B                                DKIMVerifyDetails **\fIdetails\fB);
.B const char *dkim_verify_author_domain(DKIMVerify *\fIv\fB);
.B int         dkim_verify_status(int \fInsigs\fB, const DKIMVerifyDetails *\fIdetails\fB);
.B int         dkim_verify_count(int \fInsigs\fB, const DKIMVerifyDetails *\fIdetails\fB);
.B void        dkim_verify_free(DKIMVerify *\fIv\fB);
.sp
.B const char *dkim_status_text(int \fIcode\fB, const char **\fIenhanced\fB);
.B const char *dkim_code_name(int \fIcode\fB);
.B void        dkim_free(void *\fIp\fB);
.B int         exit_if_old(int \fIcode\fB);
.fi
.sp
Link with \fB\-lcrypto\fR (OpenSSL 1.1.1 or later).
.SH DESCRIPTION
These functions, in \fIdkim_functions.c\fR, add DKIM signatures to a message
and verify the DKIM signatures of a message. They are plain C, need only libc
and OpenSSL, and replace the C++ libdkim. They are used by
\fBqmail-dkim\fR(8) and \fBdkim\fR(8).
.PP
Both signing and verifying are \fIstreaming\fR: the message is passed in with
repeated calls, in chunks of any size (including one byte), and only the
header fields are kept in memory. Line endings may be LF or CRLF. The
supported algorithms are \fBrsa\-sha1\fR, \fBrsa\-sha256\fR and
\fBed25519\-sha256\fR, with \fBsimple\fR and \fBrelaxed\fR canonicalization
for header and body.
.SS Signing
.nf
dkim_sign_new()
dkim_sign_process()  ... once per chunk of the message
dkim_sign_get()      ... once per signature wanted
dkim_sign_free()
.fi
.PP
The message is read once. While it is read the body is hashed for each of the
four combinations of canonicalization (simple, relaxed) and hash (SHA\-1,
SHA\-256), so that \fBdkim_sign_get\fR() can be called any number of times, with
a different key, selector, algorithm or canonicalization each time, without
the message being passed in again. This is how several signatures (for
example RSA and Ed25519) are made.
.SS Verifying
.nf
dkim_verify_new()
dkim_verify_process() ... once per chunk of the message
dkim_verify_finish()  ... result for every DKIM\-Signature header
dkim_verify_free()
.fi
.PP
Every DKIM\-Signature header is verified independently, with its own
canonicalization, hash algorithm, \fIl=\fR limit and key lookup. At most
\fBDKIM_MAX_VERIFY_SIGNATURES\fR (10) signatures are examined; the rest are
ignored.
.SH DATA TYPES
.SS DKIMSignOptions
Initialize with \fBmemset(&opts, 0, sizeof(opts))\fR and set what you need.
.TP
.B pfnHeaderCallback
\fBint (*)(const char *header)\fR. Called for every header field of the
message; return 1 to include it in the signature (\fIh=\fR), 0 to leave it
out. \fIheader\fR is the field as it appears in the message ("Name: value",
folded lines joined with CRLF). If NULL every header is signed. The From
header is signed whatever the callback returns. Used by \fBdkim_sign_new\fR().
.TP
.B szDomain
The \fId=\fR tag. If empty, the domain is taken from the message (see
\fBdkim_sign_domain\fR()). Used by \fBdkim_sign_new\fR().
.TP
.B szDefaultDomain
\fId=\fR to use when no domain can be found in the message. Used by
\fBdkim_sign_new\fR().
.TP
.B nCanon
Canonicalization, header/body: \fBDKIM_SIGN_SIMPLE\fR (simple/simple),
\fBDKIM_SIGN_RELAXED\fR (relaxed/relaxed), \fBDKIM_SIGN_RELAXED_SIMPLE\fR
(relaxed header, simple body) or \fBDKIM_SIGN_SIMPLE_RELAXED\fR (simple header,
relaxed body). Note that the value 0 is simple/simple. Used by
\fBdkim_sign_get\fR().
.TP
.B nHash
\fBDKIM_HASH_SHA1\fR (rsa\-sha1), \fBDKIM_HASH_SHA256\fR (rsa\-sha256),
\fBDKIM_HASH_SHA1_AND_SHA256\fR (two RSA signatures with the same key, SHA\-1
first) or \fBDKIM_HASH_ED25519\fR (ed25519\-sha256). The key must match: an RSA
key for the first three, an Ed25519 key for the last, otherwise
\fBDKIM_BAD_PRIVATE_KEY\fR is returned. SHA\-1 is deprecated by RFC 8301. Used
by \fBdkim_sign_get\fR().
.TP
.B nIncludeBodyLengthTag
Non\-zero adds \fIl=\fR (length of the canonicalized body). Used by
\fBdkim_sign_get\fR().
.TP
.B nIncludeQueryMethod
Non\-zero adds \fIq=dns/txt\fR.
.TP
.B nIncludeTimeStamp
Non\-zero adds \fIt=\fR (current time).
.TP
.B nIncludeCopiedHeaders
Non\-zero adds \fIz=\fR, a copy of the signed headers.
.TP
.B expireTime
Absolute time, as returned by \fBtime\fR(2), for \fIx=\fR. 0 means no \fIx=\fR.
.TP
.B szSelector
The \fIs=\fR tag. The public key is looked up at
\fIselector\fB._domainkey.\fIdomain\fR.
.TP
.B szIdentity
The \fIi=\fR tag, \fIlocal@domain\fR. The domain must be \fId=\fR or a
subdomain of it, otherwise \fBDKIM_BAD_IDENTITY\fR is returned. Empty means no
\fIi=\fR.
.SS DKIMVerifyOptions
.TP
.B nAllowUnsignedFromHeaders
0 (default): every From header of the message must be listed in \fIh=\fR,
otherwise the signature fails with \fBDKIM_UNSIGNED_FROM\fR. Non\-zero skips
the check.
.TP
.B nSubjectRequired
Non\-zero: if the message has a Subject header, it must be listed in \fIh=\fR,
otherwise the signature fails with \fBDKIM_BAD_SYNTAX\fR.
.TP
.B nHonorBodyLengthTag
Non\-zero: only the first \fIl=\fR bytes of the canonicalized body are hashed.
A signature that verifies but does not cover the whole body gets the result
\fBDKIM_SUCCESS_BUT_EXTRA\fR. 0 (default): \fIl=\fR is ignored and the whole
body must match. Honoring \fIl=\fR lets anyone append text to a signed
message, so leave it off unless you need it.
.TP
.B verbose
Reserved.
.TP
.B pfnDnsCallback
\fBchar *(*)(const char *name)\fR. Called with \fIselector._domainkey.domain\fR
and must return the TXT record as a string allocated so that it can be
released with \fBOPENSSL_free\fR(3), or the literal strings \fB"e=temp;"\fR
(temporary DNS failure) or \fB"e=perm;"\fR (permanent failure, record does not
exist). The library frees the returned string. Required unless
\fBpszSelectorOverride\fR is set.
.TP
.B nRejectSha1
Non\-zero: an rsa\-sha1 signature that OpenSSL refuses to verify because of the
crypto policy of the system is reported as \fBDKIM_SIGNATURE_BAD\fR instead of
\fBDKIM_ALGORITHM_NOT_ALLOWED\fR. No effect if the system allows rsa\-sha1.
.TP
.B pszSelectorOverride
If not NULL, this text is used as the key record for every signature and
\fBpfnDnsCallback\fR is not called. Useful for testing.
.SS DKIMVerifyDetails
One per DKIM\-Signature header, in the order they appear in the message.
.TP
.B nResult
>= 0: verified; < 0: failed. See \fBRETURN VALUES\fR.
.TP
.B szDomain, szSelector
The \fId=\fR and \fIs=\fR values, or NULL if the signature could not be parsed
that far.
.TP
.B szAlgorithm
"rsa\-sha1", "rsa\-sha256", "ed25519\-sha256" or NULL.
.SH FUNCTIONS
.SS dkim_sign_new()
Creates a signing context from the \fIpfnHeaderCallback\fR, \fIszDomain\fR and
\fIszDefaultDomain\fR members of \fIopts\fR. Returns NULL if memory is
exhausted, or if OpenSSL is older than 1.1.1. The options are copied; they may
change afterwards without affecting the context.
.SS dkim_sign_process()
Passes \fIlen\fR bytes of the message to the signer. Returns 0, or
\fBDKIM_OUT_OF_MEMORY\fR, \fBDKIM_FAIL\fR (header larger than 16 MB) or
\fBDKIM_INVALID_CONTEXT\fR (called after \fBdkim_sign_get\fR()).
.SS dkim_sign_domain()
Returns the domain that will be used for \fId=\fR, or NULL if none can be
found. The order is: \fIszDomain\fR; the domain of the first address in the
From, Sender, Return\-Path or X\-Bounced\-Address header, in that order;
\fIszDefaultDomain\fR. Call it after the whole message has been passed to
\fBdkim_sign_process\fR(). The pointer is valid until \fBdkim_sign_free\fR().
.SS dkim_sign_get()
Makes the signature(s) for the message that has been passed in, using the
signing members of \fIopts\fR (see above) and the unencrypted PEM private key
in the NUL terminated string \fIprivkey_pem\fR (RSA, or Ed25519 when
\fInHash\fR is \fBDKIM_HASH_ED25519\fR). On success \fI*sig\fR points to
allocated memory holding one complete header, or two with
\fBDKIM_HASH_SHA1_AND_SHA256\fR:
.PP
.RS
.nf
DKIM\-Signature: v=1; a=rsa\-sha256; c=relaxed/relaxed; d=example.com;
        s=default; ...; b=...
.fi
.RE
.PP
Every line, including the last, is terminated by LF (not CRLF), and
continuation lines begin with a tab, so the result can be written in front
of a message that uses LF line ends. (The hash is computed over the CRLF form
that every verifier uses, so the signature is valid either way.) Release
\fI*sig\fR with \fBdkim_free\fR(). On failure \fI*sig\fR is NULL. The first call
completes the message; \fBdkim_sign_process\fR() can no longer be used after
it. \fBdkim_sign_get\fR() can be called as many times as needed. Besides the
common errors, it returns \fBDKIM_NO_SENDER\fR if no domain is available,
\fBDKIM_BAD_IDENTITY\fR, \fBDKIM_BAD_PRIVATE_KEY\fR (key cannot be read, is
passphrase protected, or does not match \fInHash\fR), \fBDKIM_EVP_SIGN_FAILURE\fR
and \fBDKIM_EVP_DIGEST_FAILURE\fR.
.PP
The headers signed are those the callback accepts, always including From.
They are listed in \fIh=\fR from the bottom of the message up, once per
occurrence, so that headers that occur several times are covered correctly.
.SS dkim_sign_free()
Releases the context. NULL is allowed.
.SS dkim_verify_new()
Creates a verification context. The options are copied. Returns NULL if memory
is exhausted, or if OpenSSL is older than 1.1.1.
.SS dkim_verify_process()
Passes \fIlen\fR bytes of the message to the verifier. Returns 0, or
\fBDKIM_OUT_OF_MEMORY\fR, \fBDKIM_FAIL\fR (header larger than 16 MB) or
\fBDKIM_INVALID_CONTEXT\fR (called after \fBdkim_verify_finish\fR()).
.SS dkim_verify_finish()
Ends the message and verifies every signature: it fetches the keys, compares
the body hash and checks the signature. Returns 0 and sets \fI*nsigs\fR to the
number of DKIM\-Signature headers found (0 if none) and \fI*details\fR to an
array of that many \fBDKIMVerifyDetails\fR. The array and the strings in it
belong to the context and are valid until \fBdkim_verify_free\fR(). It can be
called again and returns the same array. A non\-zero return is an error of
the kind \fBDKIM_OUT_OF_MEMORY\fR.
.PP
A failure of a signature is not an error of this function; it is reported in
that signature's \fInResult\fR.
.SS dkim_verify_author_domain()
Returns the domain of the From header (the first address of the first From
header), or NULL if there is none or the header has not been read yet. A
signature whose \fId=\fR is neither this domain nor a parent of it, but which
verifies, has the result \fBDKIM_3PS_SIGNATURE\fR. The pointer is valid until
\fBdkim_verify_free\fR().
.SS dkim_verify_status()
Reduces the details to one status code for the message. One good signature
is enough, wherever it is among the signatures (a mailing list puts its own
signature above the author's): if the signature of the author's domain
verified the result is \fBDKIM_SUCCESS\fR, no matter how many other
signatures failed. If only signatures of other domains verified the result is
\fBDKIM_3PS_SIGNATURE\fR. If the only good signatures left part of the body
uncovered it is \fBDKIM_SUCCESS_BUT_EXTRA\fR. Otherwise the result of the
first failed signature is returned. \fBDKIM_NO_SIGNATURES\fR is returned if
\fInsigs\fR is 0. \fBDKIM_PARTIAL_SUCCESS\fR is never returned.
.SS dkim_verify_count()
Returns the number of signatures in \fIdetails\fR that verified (\fInResult\fR
>= 0). A program can use it together with \fInsigs\fR to report, for example,
"1 of 2 signatures verified" when \fBdkim_verify_status\fR() says the message
is good although other signatures failed. Returns 0 if OpenSSL is older
than 1.1.1.
.SS dkim_verify_free()
Releases the context and everything returned by \fBdkim_verify_finish\fR().
NULL is allowed.
.SS dkim_status_text()
Returns a text for \fIcode\fR suitable for a DKIM\-Status header or an error
message. \fBDKIM_SUCCESS\fR gives "good" (padded with blanks, as in earlier
versions). If \fIenhanced\fR is not NULL it is set to an RFC 3463 enhanced
status code ("X.7.5", "X.7.7", "X.3.0" ...). The result is a static string.
.SS dkim_code_name()
Returns the short name of \fIcode\fR, e.g. "SIGNATURE BAD", as a static
string.
.SS dkim_free()
Releases memory returned by this library (the signature from
\fBdkim_sign_get\fR()).
.SS exit_if_old()
Returns 0. If the library was compiled with OpenSSL older than 1.1.1 it calls
\fB_exit\fR(\fIcode\fR) instead. Call it first in \fBmain\fR().
.SH RETURN VALUES
Functions that return \fIint\fR return 0 (\fBDKIM_SUCCESS\fR) on success or a
negative error code. Verification results (\fBnResult\fR, and the value of
\fBdkim_verify_status\fR()) are >= 0 for success and < 0 for failure. The letter
is the one used in the \fBDKIMVERIFY\fR environment variable of
\fBqmail-dkim\fR(8): upper case for a permanent, lower case for a temporary
error.
.PP
.na
.TS
box;
l l lw(20).
Code	Value	Meaning
